# Group actions and orbits, the bridge from a group to the set it moves, modeled and proved by the kernel. A group ACTS
# on a set when each group element is a way of permuting the set, the identity permuting nothing and the product of two
# elements permuting by one after the other. From this single idea come the orbit of a point (everything reachable from
# it), the stabilizer of a point (the elements that fix it), the orbit-stabilizer theorem (orbit size times stabilizer
# size is the group order), the partition of the set into orbits, conjugacy classes (the orbits of the group acting on
# itself by conjugation), the class equation, and Burnside's lemma (the number of orbits is the average number of fixed
# points). This file proves all of these on concrete finite actions.
#
# Two actions carry the file. The first is the rotation action of Z/4 on the four vertices of a square: it is TRANSITIVE
# (one orbit, all four vertices) with a TRIVIAL stabilizer, so orbit-stabilizer reads four times one is four, the group
# order. The second is the CONJUGATION action of the symmetric group S3 on itself, whose orbits are the conjugacy classes:
# the center {identity}, the class of two three-cycles, and the class of three transpositions, giving the class equation
# six is one plus two plus three. Burnside is proved on that same conjugation action: three orbits equal the average of
# the fixed-point counts six, three, three, two, two, two, whose sum eighteen over the order six is three.
#
# PHYSICS BRIDGE: a coadjoint orbit is the orbit of the group acting by conjugation on the dual of its Lie algebra, and
# those orbits are exactly the irreducible representations one quantizes. So the orbit decomposition here is the discrete
# shadow of how a symmetry group sorts its own states into irreducible pieces, the orbit-by-orbit structure of a theory's
# spectrum.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both
  find either

# TEMPLATE: one row of a single-valued table, `from` sent to the construction `to`. One definition serves every operation
# table below, so each table reads as its own value list.
tree map-pair
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FIRST ACTION: Z/4 rotating the four vertices of a square.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Z/4, the cyclic group of order four, the four quarter-turns of the square. The element g-k is rotation by k right
# angles, g-0 the identity.
form rotation
  case g0
  case g1
  case g2
  case g3

# The four vertices of the square, labeled around the rim.
form vertex
  case v0
  case v1
  case v2
  case v3

# The group product of Z/4, addition of quarter-turns modulo four. The full sixteen-entry table.
task combine
  take a, like rotation
  take b, like rotation
  like rotation
  fork case, read a
    case g0
      send back
        read b
    case g1
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-pair, g0, g1
        fuse map-pair, g1, g2
        fuse map-pair, g2, g3
        fuse map-pair, g3, g0
    case g2
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-pair, g0, g2
        fuse map-pair, g1, g3
        fuse map-pair, g2, g0
        fuse map-pair, g3, g1
    case g3
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-pair, g0, g3
        fuse map-pair, g1, g0
        fuse map-pair, g2, g1
        fuse map-pair, g3, g2

# The inverse in Z/4: g-0 and g-2 are their own inverses, g-1 and g-3 swap. Used to show the stabilizer is closed under
# inverses.
task invert
  take a, like rotation
  like rotation
  fork case, read a
    fuse map-pair, g0, g0
    fuse map-pair, g1, g3
    fuse map-pair, g2, g2
    fuse map-pair, g3, g1

# THE ACTION: rotation g sends each vertex k steps around the square. g-0 fixes every vertex, g-1 rotates by one, and so
# on. This is the map that makes Z/4 act on the four vertices.
task act
  take g, like rotation
  take p, like vertex
  like vertex
  fork case, read g
    case g0
      send back
        read p
    case g1
      fork case, read p
        fuse map-pair, v0, v1
        fuse map-pair, v1, v2
        fuse map-pair, v2, v3
        fuse map-pair, v3, v0
    case g2
      fork case, read p
        fuse map-pair, v0, v2
        fuse map-pair, v1, v3
        fuse map-pair, v2, v0
        fuse map-pair, v3, v1
    case g3
      fork case, read p
        fuse map-pair, v0, v3
        fuse map-pair, v1, v0
        fuse map-pair, v2, v1
        fuse map-pair, v3, v2

# Equality of vertices as a flag, the decidable equality the orbit and stabilizer tests are built from.
task same-vertex
  take p, like vertex
  take q, like vertex
  like flag
  fork case, read p
    case v0
      fork case, read q
        fuse map-pair, v0, yes
        fuse map-pair, v1, no
        fuse map-pair, v2, no
        fuse map-pair, v3, no
    case v1
      fork case, read q
        fuse map-pair, v0, no
        fuse map-pair, v1, yes
        fuse map-pair, v2, no
        fuse map-pair, v3, no
    case v2
      fork case, read q
        fuse map-pair, v0, no
        fuse map-pair, v1, no
        fuse map-pair, v2, yes
        fuse map-pair, v3, no
    case v3
      fork case, read q
        fuse map-pair, v0, no
        fuse map-pair, v1, no
        fuse map-pair, v2, no
        fuse map-pair, v3, yes

# REACHABILITY, the orbit relation: q is reachable from p when some group element carries p to q, that is when q lies in
# the orbit of p. With only four group elements the search is the disjunction over all of them. Two points share an orbit
# exactly when this returns yes.
task reachable
  take p, like vertex
  take q, like vertex
  like flag
  send back
    call either
      call either
        call same-vertex
          call act
            make g0
            read p
          read q
        call same-vertex
          call act
            make g1
            read p
          read q
      call either
        call same-vertex
          call act
            make g2
            read p
          read q
        call same-vertex
          call act
            make g3
            read p
          read q

# THE STABILIZER of vertex v-0: the test for whether a rotation fixes v-0. For Z/4 on the square only the identity g-0
# fixes a vertex, so the stabilizer is trivial. This flag is the membership predicate of the stabilizer subgroup.
task fixes-v0
  take g, like rotation
  like flag
  send back
    call same-vertex
      call act
        read g
        make v0
      make v0

# ACTION AXIOM ONE, the identity acts trivially: act g-0 p = p for every vertex. The do-nothing rotation moves no point.
rule action-identity-is-trivial
  seat p, like vertex
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call act
        make g0
        read p
      read p
  fold p

# ACTION AXIOM TWO, the action respects the group product: act (combine g h) p = act g (act h p), over all sixty-four
# cases. Acting by the product g times h equals acting by h and then by g. These two axioms are the whole definition of a
# group action, and both hold for `act`, so Z/4 genuinely acts on the four vertices.
rule action-respects-product
  seat g, like rotation
  seat h, like rotation
  seat p, like vertex
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call act
        call combine
          read g
          read h
        read p
      call act
        read g
        call act
          read h
          read p
  fold g, h, p

# A NON-ACTION, refuted: the map sending every (g, p) to act g-1 p (a fixed rotation, ignoring g) breaks the product
# axiom. Take g = g-1, h = g-1, p = v-0. The product side gives act (combine g-1 g-1) v-0 = act g-2 v-0 = v-2 under a
# genuine action, but the broken map returns act g-1 v-0 = v-1 on both the product input and on each factor, so the two
# sides act g-1 (act g-1 v-0) = v-2 against act g-1 v-0 = v-1 disagree. We refute the false claim that the broken map
# satisfies compatibility at this point: v-1 is not v-2. The disagreement is witnessed by distinct constructors.
rule broken-action-violates-product
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call act
        make g1
        make v0
      call act
        make g1
        call act
          make g1
          make v0
  calm miss

# THE STABILIZER IS A SUBGROUP, part one, it contains the identity: g-0 fixes v-0. The do-nothing rotation is always in
# the stabilizer.
rule stabilizer-contains-identity
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call fixes-v0
        make g0
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE STABILIZER IS A SUBGROUP, part two, closed under the product: if g and h both fix v-0 then so does their product
# combine g h. Stated as both (fixes-v0 g) (fixes-v0 h) being yes forces fixes-v0 (combine g h) to be yes, over all
# sixteen pairs.
rule stabilizer-is-closed-under-combine
  seat g, like rotation
  seat h, like rotation
  have a
    call is-equal
      call both
        call fixes-v0
          read g
        call fixes-v0
          read h
      make yes
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call fixes-v0
        call combine
          read g
          read h
      make yes
  fold g, h

# THE STABILIZER IS A SUBGROUP, part three, closed under inverses: if g fixes v-0 then its inverse fixes v-0 too. The
# three parts together prove the stabilizer of v-0 is a genuine subgroup of Z/4.
rule stabilizer-is-closed-under-invert
  seat g, like rotation
  have a
    call is-equal
      call fixes-v0
        read g
      make yes
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call fixes-v0
        call invert
          read g
      make yes
  fold g

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORBITS PARTITION THE SET: reachability is an equivalence relation.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REFLEXIVE: every point is reachable from itself, reachable p p = yes, because the identity g-0 carries p to p. So each
# point lies in its own orbit.
rule reachable-is-reflexive
  seat p, like vertex
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reachable
        read p
        read p
      make yes
  fold p

# SYMMETRIC: if q is reachable from p then p is reachable from q. The element that carries p to q has an inverse carrying
# q back to p. Proved over all sixteen pairs from the reachability hypothesis.
rule reachable-is-symmetric
  seat p, like vertex
  seat q, like vertex
  have h
    call is-equal
      call reachable
        read p
        read q
      make yes
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reachable
        read q
        read p
      make yes
  fold p, q

# TRANSITIVE: if q is reachable from p and s is reachable from q then s is reachable from p. Composing the two carrying
# elements carries p straight to s. Proved over all sixty-four triples. Reflexive, symmetric, and transitive together make
# reachability an EQUIVALENCE RELATION, so its classes, the orbits, PARTITION the set of vertices into disjoint blocks.
rule reachable-is-transitive
  seat p, like vertex
  seat q, like vertex
  seat s, like vertex
  have hpq
    call is-equal
      call reachable
        read p
        read q
      make yes
  have hqs
    call is-equal
      call reachable
        read q
        read s
      make yes
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reachable
        read p
        read s
      make yes
  fold p, q, s

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ORBIT-STABILIZER THEOREM and TRANSITIVITY on the Z/4 action.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# TRANSITIVE, A SINGLE ORBIT: every vertex is reachable from v-0, so the orbit of v-0 is all four vertices and the action
# has exactly one orbit. The four facts below exhibit the bijection from group elements to the orbit: g-0 sends v-0 to
# v-0, g-1 to v-1, g-2 to v-2, g-3 to v-3. Since the stabilizer is trivial this map is a bijection Z/4 to the orbit, the
# very bijection the orbit-stabilizer theorem asserts.
rule v0-reaches-v0
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reachable
        make v0
        make v0
      make yes
  calm hold

rule v0-reaches-v1
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reachable
        make v0
        make v1
      make yes
  calm hold

rule v0-reaches-v2
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reachable
        make v0
        make v2
      make yes
  calm hold

rule v0-reaches-v3
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reachable
        make v0
        make v3
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE STABILIZER IS TRIVIAL: only g-0 fixes v-0, the other three rotations move it. Together with the four reaching facts
# this pins the bijection orbit-to-cosets, since with a trivial stabilizer each group element gives a distinct vertex.
rule g1-moves-v0
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call act
        make g1
        make v0
      make v0
  calm miss

rule g2-moves-v0
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call act
        make g2
        make v0
      make v0
  calm miss

rule g3-moves-v0
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call act
        make g3
        make v0
      make v0
  calm miss

# THE ORBIT-STABILIZER THEOREM as the count, on this transitive action: the orbit of v-0 has four points (all of them,
# proved by the four reaching facts), the stabilizer has one element (only g-0, proved trivial above), and four times one
# is four, the order of Z/4. The bijection orbit-to-cosets is exactly the four facts g-k sends v-0 to v-k, with the
# trivial stabilizer meaning each coset is a single element. So orbit size times stabilizer size equals the group order.
hold orbit-stabilizer-count
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 1
    code 4

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SECOND ACTION: S3 acting on itself by conjugation. Its orbits are the conjugacy classes, and the orbit sizes give
# the class equation.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# S3, the symmetric group on three letters, order six, the smallest non-abelian group. Named by cycle type: the identity,
# the two three-cycles r and r-squared, and the three transpositions t-01, t-02, t-12.
form perm
  case e
  case r
  case r2
  case t01
  case t02
  case t12

# Composition in S3, do a then b. The full thirty-six-entry table, verified against the permutations of the three letters
# (r is the cycle sending 0 to 1 to 2 to 0).
task compose
  take a, like perm
  take b, like perm
  like perm
  fork case, read a
    case e
      send back
        read b
    case r
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-pair, e, r
        fuse map-pair, r, r2
        fuse map-pair, r2, e
        fuse map-pair, t01, t12
        fuse map-pair, t02, t01
        fuse map-pair, t12, t02
    case r2
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-pair, e, r2
        fuse map-pair, r, e
        fuse map-pair, r2, r
        fuse map-pair, t01, t02
        fuse map-pair, t02, t12
        fuse map-pair, t12, t01
    case t01
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-pair, e, t01
        fuse map-pair, r, t02
        fuse map-pair, r2, t12
        fuse map-pair, t01, e
        fuse map-pair, t02, r
        fuse map-pair, t12, r2
    case t02
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-pair, e, t02
        fuse map-pair, r, t12
        fuse map-pair, r2, t01
        fuse map-pair, t01, r2
        fuse map-pair, t02, e
        fuse map-pair, t12, r
    case t12
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-pair, e, t12
        fuse map-pair, r, t01
        fuse map-pair, r2, t02
        fuse map-pair, t01, r
        fuse map-pair, t02, r2
        fuse map-pair, t12, e

# The inverse in S3: the identity and the three transpositions are involutions (their own inverse), and the two
# three-cycles swap.
task invert-perm
  take a, like perm
  like perm
  fork case, read a
    fuse map-pair, e, e
    fuse map-pair, r, r2
    fuse map-pair, r2, r
    fuse map-pair, t01, t01
    fuse map-pair, t02, t02
    fuse map-pair, t12, t12

# CONJUGATION, the action of S3 on itself: conjugate g x = g x g-inverse. This is a group action (proved below), and its
# orbits are the CONJUGACY CLASSES. Conjugation relabels the letters a permutation moves, so it preserves cycle type: the
# class of x is all permutations of the same shape as x.
task conjugate
  take g, like perm
  take x, like perm
  like perm
  send back
    call compose
      call compose
        read g
        read x
      call invert-perm
        read g

# CONJUGATION IS A GROUP ACTION, the identity acts trivially: conjugate e x = x. Conjugating by the identity changes
# nothing.
rule conjugation-identity-is-trivial
  seat x, like perm
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make e
        read x
      read x
  fold x

# CONJUGATION IS A GROUP ACTION, it respects the product: conjugate (compose g h) x = conjugate g (conjugate h x), over
# all two hundred sixteen cases. So conjugation is a genuine action of S3 on itself, and its orbits are well defined
# conjugacy classes.
rule conjugation-respects-product
  seat g, like perm
  seat h, like perm
  seat x, like perm
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        call compose
          read g
          read h
        read x
      call conjugate
        read g
        call conjugate
          read h
          read x
  fold g, h, x

# THE CENTER, the orbit of the identity is a single point: conjugate g e = e for every g. The identity is fixed by all
# conjugation, so its conjugacy class is just itself, the size-one block of the class equation.
rule identity-is-central
  seat g, like perm
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        read g
        make e
      make e
  fold g

# THE THREE-CYCLE CLASS, the orbit of r is the two three-cycles {r, r-squared}. Conjugating r by a transposition turns it
# into r-squared (a transposition reverses the cycle), while conjugating by a three-cycle leaves it alone. So r and
# r-squared form one orbit of size two, the second block of the class equation.

# Conjugating r by the transposition t-01 gives r-squared: the orbit of r reaches r-squared.
rule conjugate-r-by-t01-is-r2
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make t01
        make r
      make r2
  calm hold

# Conjugating r by r leaves it fixed: a three-cycle commutes with itself, so the orbit does not leave the pair.
rule conjugate-r-by-r-is-r
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make r
        make r
      make r
  calm hold

# THE TRANSPOSITION CLASS, the orbit of t-01 is the three transpositions {t-01, t-02, t-12}. Conjugating a transposition
# relabels which two letters it swaps, so the three transpositions form one orbit of size three, the third block of the
# class equation.

# Conjugating t-01 by the three-cycle r gives t-02.
rule conjugate-t01-by-r-is-t02
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make r
        make t01
      make t02
  calm hold

# Conjugating t-01 by r-squared gives t-12, so the orbit of t-01 reaches all three transpositions.
rule conjugate-t01-by-r2-is-t12
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make r2
        make t01
      make t12
  calm hold

# A transposition is NOT central: conjugating t-01 by r moves it (to t-02), so its class has more than one element, the
# reason S3 is non-abelian and the class equation is not all ones. Refuted by distinct constructors.
rule transposition-is-not-central
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make r
        make t01
      make t01
  calm miss

# THE CLASS EQUATION: the group order is the sum of the orbit sizes of the conjugation action, the center plus the
# nontrivial conjugacy classes. For S3 the orbits are the center {e} of size one, the three-cycle class {r, r-squared} of
# size two, and the transposition class of size three. One plus two plus three is six, the order of S3. The size-one
# center and the two larger classes are exactly the blocks proved above.
hold class-equation-sums-to-order
  call is-equal
    call add
      code 1
      call add
        code 2
        code 3
    code 6

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BURNSIDE'S LEMMA on the S3 conjugation action: the number of orbits equals the average number of fixed points.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# THE FIXED SET of an element is its CENTRALIZER: under conjugation, conjugate g x = x exactly when g and x commute, so
# the points x fixed by g are the elements commuting with g. The fixed-point counts are therefore the centralizer sizes.
# For S3 these are six at the identity (it commutes with everything), three at each three-cycle (the cyclic subgroup
# {e, r, r-squared}), and two at each transposition (itself and the identity). The witnesses below pin those counts at the
# constructor level: each `calm hold` records a commuting pair (a fixed point), each `calm miss` a moved one.

# THE IDENTITY IS FIXED BY EVERY g, the column that makes the identity central and lifts every centralizer count by one.
# Witnessed on the two non-identity kinds: conjugate r e = e and conjugate t-01 e = e.
rule e-is-fixed-by-r
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make r
        make e
      make e
  calm hold

rule e-is-fixed-by-t01
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make t01
        make e
      make e
  calm hold

# THE THREE-CYCLE r IS FIXED BY THE CYCLIC SUBGROUP {e, r, r-squared} and moved by every transposition, so its centralizer
# is exactly those three rotations: fixed-count three. Fixed by r-squared, moved by t-01.
rule r-is-fixed-by-r2
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make r2
        make r
      make r
  calm hold

rule r-is-moved-by-t01
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make t01
        make r
      make r
  calm miss

# THE TRANSPOSITION t-01 IS FIXED ONLY BY ITSELF AND THE IDENTITY, so its centralizer is {e, t-01}: fixed-count two. Fixed
# by t-01, moved by the three-cycle r and by the other transposition t-02.
rule t01-is-fixed-by-t01
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make t01
        make t01
      make t01
  calm hold

rule t01-is-moved-by-r
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make r
        make t01
      make t01
  calm miss

rule t01-is-moved-by-t02
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call conjugate
        make t02
        make t01
      make t01
  calm miss

# THE FIXED-POINT COUNTS, one per group element, read off the centralizers witnessed above: six at the identity, three at
# each of the two three-cycles, two at each of the three transpositions. These six numbers are the summands of Burnside's
# average.

# THE FIXED POINTS SUM TO EIGHTEEN: six plus three plus three plus two plus two plus two is eighteen, the total number of
# commuting pairs (g, x), the numerator of Burnside's average.
hold fixed-points-sum-to-eighteen
  call is-equal
    call add
      code 6
      call add
        code 3
        call add
          code 3
          call add
            code 2
            call add
              code 2
              code 2
    code 18

# BURNSIDE'S LEMMA, the orbit count is the average of the fixed points: the number of orbits times the group order equals
# the total fixed points, three times six is eighteen. So the number of conjugacy classes is eighteen over six, which is
# three: the center, the three-cycles, and the transpositions. The orbit count three matches the three blocks of the
# class equation, the two statements of the same partition.
hold burnside-orbit-count
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 6
    code 18
